From dd8d49d5d3be39f4f0740f3cc463da30a54d7caf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 19:17:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] server/llama : device checkpoint storage-ID ownership +
 quantized device-view extent (R45, lane-229)

Two defects in the R44 device-resident context-checkpoint path, both of which corrupt a restored checkpoint on the drafted seat line. The first aborts the server; the second is silent.

1. tools/server/server-context.cpp ctx_ckpt_flags() handed out storage ids from a bare modulo counter, 1 + (ckpt_storage_next++ % n_ring), on the premise that the live checkpoint list only ever holds the last n created entries. It does not: thinning keeps the FIRST entry and exempts tool-call anchors, and prefix invalidation erases NEWER entries. The counter therefore wraps onto an id a live checkpoint still owns, the new save overwrites that checkpoint's device buffers under the same storage key, and restoring the old metadata reads the new buffers - the "~llama_io_read_device: memory buffer mismatch" abort at task 594 of the R44 c1 sanity run when the totals differ, and a silent wrong-state restore when they happen to match. server_ckpt_storage_alloc() (new header tools/server/server-ckpt-storage.h) allocates only an id no live ON_DEVICE checkpoint owns, scanning after eviction and after the new entry is appended, which is where create_checkpoint calls it. It returns 0 - fall back to a host image - when the ring exceeds the 255 usable storage ids or every id is owned; with ring = n_ctx_checkpoints + 1 and a list capped at n_ctx_checkpoints entries, an id is always free.

2. src/llama-context.cpp built its device views with bytes/ggml_element_size(). For a quantized tensor that is the BLOCK size, so the expression yields blocks while ggml_view_1d and ggml_new_tensor_1d take an ELEMENT count. With a q8_0 KV cache every device-checkpoint view was 1/32 of the intended extent: the image carried a fraction of the cells and the restore silently returned mostly stale KV. f16/f32 have block size 1, which is why every existing device test - all of them f16 - passed. The seat line runs q8_0 K/V.

Also hardening: llama_io_read_device's validation and copy moved out of the destructor into an explicit commit(), called by state_seq_set_data only after state_seq_read_data returns cleanly, so a failed deserialization can no longer copy a partial request set or abort the process during cleanup. The size-guard abort now names the branch and both byte totals; the R44 crash logs had neither.

tests: tests/test-ctx-checkpoint-storage.cpp is a pure allocator regression - Astra's capacity-32 counterexample plus the anchor, invalidation, capacity, two-slot and reserved-id-0 variants. It drives both allocators, so one run receipts RED (R44 reuses live id 1 at creation 34) and GREEN (R45 holds). test-save-load-state gains Test 10: save A at 40 cells under an allocator-issued id, advance to 60, rotate past a full ring while A stays live, restore A and compare against a host-image control - f16 and q8_0, device and host-control legs. 54 of 54 generated archs pass. LLAMA_R45_RED_QUANT_VIEW=1 restores the old view math for one run so the q8_0 RED stays reproducible without a rebuild.

Origin: fork-authored (ArifiLabs lane-229 R45, Opus 5 MEDIUM, kernel-maker); repairs the ring introduced by ea100547f (R31 P1/P2, FreeToken M14/M15); crash analysis ASTRA-R44-CRASH-ANALYSIS.md
Measured-effect: UNMEASURED for speed - correctness only. Fixes the reproducible R44 abort at task 594 of the c1 drafted sanity run (FIX-df2-L2/L5); served A/B in the lane-229 report
Lane: lane-229
Conflict-resolved: hand: test-save-load-state.cpp - Test 10 storage-id ownership (4 legs: f16/q8_0 x host-control/device) registered as upstream-suite column 'own' (all four must pass); Test 9 already in the suite from stop 573
